<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yokoi Shōnan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yokoi Shōnan was a Japanese Confucian scholar and political reformer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> domain. Invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yoshinaga">Matsudaira Yoshinaga</a> in 1858, he promoted trade, Western technology and government by council, and his ideas influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-oath">Charter Oath</a> of 1868. He served the new Meiji government as a councillor but was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1869 by conservatives who accused him of favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and republicanism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yokoi-shonan.jpg" alt="Yokoi Shōnan" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yokoi_Shonan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
